From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Mon, 17 Oct 1994 10:47:45 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-275] BABY: Re: The Procreation Instinct

At 8:49 AM 10/16/94, Nick Szabo wrote:
>My thanks to Tim Starr for his interesting excerpts from
>_Baby Hunger_.  He adds:
>
>> such instinct will soon select themselves out of the gene pool
>>given access to  effective contraception.
>
>Is there evidence that these emotions do in fact make women less
>likely to use contraception?  There is no instictual connection
>between attraction to babies and the activity of discontuing birth
>control -- making that connection requires abstract,
>conscious decision making.  There is no short term satiation
>from removing the birth control from which the emotional
>mind could connect removing the birth control with having
>a baby, in the way there is short term satation of chocolate
>craving from buying chocolate, of sleepiness from laying
>down on a comfy bed, etc.  There is no chance for the
>behavioral learning that dominates at the emotional level to
>kick in and turn craving for babies into a decision to remove
>birth control.  If the emotional mind doesn't make that connection,
>then abstract decision-making cognition, dominated by memes, has
>no difficulty in overriding the "hunger".

Sorry, Nick; this paragraph isn't up to your usual intellectual standard.
The emotional mind doesn't need to make any abstract connection, because
the abstract, rational mind is the slave of the emotional mind:  whatever
Lola wants, Lola will figure out a way to get.  To use your example, the
craving for chocolate motivates the intellectual, abstract process of
trekking to the ATM machine, locating an open 7-11, etc.  A woman's
rational mind knows what makes babies and what needs to be done to enable
baby-making; driven by the arational imperatives of the emotional mind, the
logical mind is capable of exactly the kind of long-term planning (quitting
the Pill; seducing a sperm donor; etc.) that will result in pregnancy and
childbirth.  Humans frequently make long-term plans for satiation of
emotional cravings (the craving to build cities, to amass capital, to live
in comfort, to be free of coercion); that's what humans do.

>In any case, it seems quite
>prone to memetic short-circuit, as the demographic statistics
>of populations with high access to and ability to use birth
>control dramatically show.  Memes that influence the decision
>in favor of birth control have and will evolve far faster than
>the genes for mothering instinct can evolve into genes for baby
>hunger.

This presumes that "baby hunger" is an intellectual-rational response to an
underlying emotional-biological "mothering instinct".  I think the point of
the book Tim cited was that the "baby hunger" itself is an aspect of that
emotional-biological imperative, not a separate thing.  The slowness of
genetic evolution works in favor of "baby hunger", because genes trump
memes -- I can't "convince" you to become diabetic (or, to use a more
genetic-behavioral example, homosexual); your biochemistry is not
susceptible to memetic persuasion.

[Let's not open up a debate on the origins of sexual orientation, please;
sure, there's room for debate, but most people are "instictively" certain
that they couldn't just "decide" or "be persuaded" to flip their
hetero/homo bit based purely on ideation.]

