From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 17 Oct 94 15:54:18 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-276] BOOK: The Bell Curve (Any thoughts?)

A new book by the sociologist, Charles Murray and the late psychologist, Richard
Herrnstein called "The Bell Curve:  Intelligence and Class Structure in American
Life" is out now.  The authors seem to think that the difference between the
"cognitive elite" and the "cognitively challenged" is getting larger and larger,
much of it due to the ability of the aforesaid elite to become more powerful by
use of sophisticated technology that the simpletons can't figure out.

Has anyone actually read this yet?  Or read reviews about it and have any
thoughts?  Who, oh who could this "cognitive elite" be?  Anyone we might know?

>From the reactions and reviews I have read so far, I would have to agree that IQ
tests measure maybe only a third of what makes for cognitive excellence.  I am
hoping that what David Gelernter refers to as a social intelligence test (in his
book "The Muse in the Machine"), will be developed soon.  It will measure our
"emotional acuity quotient" or our ability to detect patterns of  extremely fine
distinctions in world events of a non-analytical nature.  Standard IQ tests
measure mostly for analytical reasoning.  The last third of our cognitive
excellence is creativity and vision deriving from low-focus thought processes,
frequently the source of genius.  The authors don't offer any changes to our
political structure, but they seem to think that this trend means the eventual
end of our current structure and that this would be bad.

By making such blatant references to the inequality that anyone can plainly see
by looking around, and to the moderate genetic influence on intelligence, the
authors have incurred the never-ending wrath of the entropic death-worshippers
who are apoplectic over this book's implications.  Their critics maintain that
we all must pretend that we are equal and that the topic of cognitive inequality
is off-limits to scientific study.  My own observation is that the only equality
in nature, is the equality of the graveyard.  This is why everyone who espouses
equality gets categorized as a death-worshipper, because equality is only
achieved in death, and the policies of the death-worshippers invariably drive
society towards the graveyard.

These authors are brave, however incomplete their data or conclusions are.

