From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 17 Oct 1994 13:29:12 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-277] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

Michael Clive Price writes:
>What I meant was that whilst the operational (frequentist) definition
>of probability only becomes precise in the limit as number of trials
>goes to infinity that doesn't stop probability being meaningful for
>finite applications.  Similarly with the derivation of probabilities
>with many-worlds.  It only becomes rigorous in the infinite limit, but
>that need not stop us from making probabilistic statements in finite
>cases.  For instance, how do you test the statement that a particular
>coin is unbiased?  The only way I know is to toss it repeatedly.  Only
>as the number of trials goes to infinity we can gain complete assurance
>that the coin is fair (or not).

Most standard treatments of statistics (say a graduate text for a
Statistics department) do not invoke any "operational definition" of
probability.  They include many tests, such as for coin randomness.
No, these tests do not offer complete assurance, but few things do.
Standard statistics is just as rigorous in finite as in infinite
cases, and doesn't look much different in the two cases.

Many-Worlds, in contrast, looks very different in the finite and
infinite cases.  In the infinite case, almost all worlds have
histories which vindicate quantum statistics.  In the finite case,
almost all worlds have histories which vindicate equi-probable
statistics.  Since our universe seems finite, this seems a problem to
me.  There is no analogous problem in general statistics: finite
histories of coin tosses have the same qualitative features as
infinite histories.

Robin Hanson

