From: sw@tiac.net (Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 17 Oct 1994 18:33:01 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-279] The Procreation Instinct Myth

Nick Szabo just posted this to the exi-essay list:

>                The Procreation Instinct Myth
>                Copyright (c) 1994 by Nick Szabo
>        permission to redistribute without alteration hereby
>        granted

>...Effective birth control, firmly in the
>service of memes, utterly trumps any genetic "procreation instinct".
>
>Unlike the pessimistic population control advocates, I do not
>not find this fact to be good news.  Rather I am profoundly
>disturbed by this phenomenon; I find it a threat to extropian
>goals fully rivalling statism and deathism.  Until reliable
>birth control in the developed cultures of the 20th century,
>the extropian value of boundless expansion emerged
>automatically...

I'm not interested in boundless expansion.  Sure, I don't want any
unnecessary limits put on "expansion," but expansion in itself is not
a value to me.  Population control is not pessimism to me.  I like
the best, not necessarily the most.

Any meme, gene, process, method, whatever, that allows procreation and
carries itself along for the ride, is going to cause a tendency for
population to increase exponentially.

Nick's concern seems to be that if the smart people have birth control,
they'll collectively choose to become an ever-shrinking minority.
But with indefinite lifespans, this is only a problem in proportion to
other people who spend more time procreating.  If that is a problem at
all, then maybe the solution is to help those other people reach the
same longevity, choice, and rationality in their own lives.

 --Steve

