From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Mon, 17 Oct 1994 16:30:38 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-283] BABY: Re: The Procreation Instinct

Dave Krieger:
>The craving for chocolate motivates the intellectual, abstract
>process of trekking to the ATM machine, locating an open 7-11, etc.

There are at least two big differences: the reward is almost immediate, 
rather than nine months removed from the necessary behavior, 
and the chocolate craving can occur and be satiated several
times a week rather than a few times in a lifetime.  It is
far easier to connect behavior to outcome the first time,
and it is far easier to repeat the experiment to obtain confidence
in that hypothesized connection, or change hypotheses if the
first was in error.  These characteristics make chocolate craving 
far more connectable to learned chocolate satiation behavior 
than than "baby hunger" is to a learned behavior needed having a 
baby, like stopping use of birth control.  

My own experience is that, alas for my health, I get chocolate 
cravings all the time, but the only times I can recall using an 
ATM to specifically satisfy a craving was when it was in the store.  
Memetically I'm quite opposed to ingesting so much chocolate, but that 
usually only overrides the cravings when there is no money in my 
pocket or ATM in the store. :-)  If I have no immediate outlet for 
the craving the memetic override works fine.

Furthermore, "baby hunger" can be satiated by much more 
immediate and accessible feedback, in the form of adopting and 
caring for a cute pet or even a cuddly toy.  Thus "baby hunger" 
can be short circuited, just as birth control short circuits 
the connection between heterosexual desire and making babies.

Finally, I again refer folks to the demographic literature,
which shows quite clearly and overwhelmingly the self-genocide
phenomenon.  Every significant culture that has access to, doesn't
discourage the use of, and is skilled in the use of effective
birth control has substantially negative population growth rate,
without exception, across a wide variety cultural origins and
other cultural patterns.  Whatever "baby hunger" there might be 
doesn't come anywhere close to making up for the impact of birth 
control, or even returning net population growth rate in these 
cultures back up to zero.

>I think the point of the book Tim cited was that the "baby hunger" 
>itself is an aspect of that emotional-biological imperative, not
>a separate thing.

That may have been its point, but it was hardly demonstrated.
Nor is does it seem to be accepted, or even deemed important, by 
most psychologists.  On the other hand, sex dominates the 
psychological literature as well as popular culture;
clearly sex was the dominant motivator of genetic reproduction, 
with "baby hunger" a tiny notion in comparison, if it existed 
at all.

Nick Szabo				szaob@netcom.com

